{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.11.13","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[{"sourceId":647395,"sourceType":"modelInstanceVersion","isSourceIdPinned":true,"modelInstanceId":488272,"modelId":503696}],"dockerImageVersionId":31192,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"source":"<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/code/masoudshahrian/converting-a-pytorch-pt-model-to-ncnn?scriptVersionId=280897696\" target=\"_blank\"><img align=\"left\" alt=\"Kaggle\" title=\"Open in Kaggle\" src=\"https://kaggle.com/static/images/open-in-kaggle.svg\"></a>","metadata":{},"cell_type":"markdown"},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Converting a PyTorch (.pt) Model to NCNN Format for Raspberry Pi 5 # ","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Converting a PyTorch model***** (typically saved as a **.pt** file) to **NCNN** (**.param** and **.bin** files) is a common optimization step for deploying inference on resource-constrained devices like the **Raspberry Pi5**.** NCNN** is lightweight, supports ARM architecture (aarch64), and can leverage the Pi 5's CPU efficiently, often achieving 3-8 FPS for models like **YOLO** depending on complexity and optimizations like quantization.\n\nThe process generally involves exporting the PyTorch model to an intermediate format (like **ONNX** or TorchScript) and then to **NCNN.** The most reliable modern method uses pnnx (from the **NCNN** project), which converts directly from PyTorch/TorchScript, bypassing common **ONNX** issues like unsupported operators or dynamic shapes. For YOLO models (e.g., **Ultralytics YOLOv8/YOLO11**), the built-in ultralytics exporter simplifies this further.\nNote: Conversion is typically done on a more powerful machine (e.g., x86 PC with **Ubuntu/Windows**) with PyTorch installed, then transfer the ***.param*** and **.*bin***** files to the Pi 5. On the Pi 5, you'll need **NCNN** installed for inference (see below).","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Prerequisites","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"* Python 3.8+ and PyTorch 1.8+ (**install via pip install torch torchvision**).\n* For pnnx: **pip install pnnx**.\n* For YOLO-specific: **pip install ultralytics**.\n* Git for cloning repos if needed.\n\nOn Raspberry Pi 5 (for inference):\n     * Install NCNN: Follow a guide like Q-engineering's NCNN on Pi 5. Key steps:\n          1. Update system: sudo apt update && sudo apt install cmake libvulkan-dev vulkan-tools.\n          2. Clone NCNN: git clone https://github.com/Tencent/ncnn.git.\n          3. Build: cd ncnn && mkdir build && cd build && cmake -DNCNN_DISABLE_RTTI=OFF -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release .. && make -j4 && sudo make install.\n\n          * This enables full functionality with OpenCV if needed.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Method 1: Direct Conversion Using pnnx (Recommended for General PyTorch Models)","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"pnnx is NCNN's official tool for PyTorch → NCNN, handling most operators reliably. It's faster and more stable than the old PyTorch → ONNX → NCNN pipeline.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# **1. **Prepare Your Model:****\n\nLoad and trace your model to TorchScript (.pt must be scriptable; if it's just state_dict, load it into the model architecture first).","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport pnnx\n\n# Example: Load your custom model (replace with your architecture)\nclass MyModel(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super(MyModel, self).__init__()\n        self.conv1 = nn.Conv2d(3, 16, 3, 1, 1)\n        self.relu = nn.ReLU()\n        self.fc = nn.Linear(16 * 224 * 224, 10)  # Adjust for your model\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        x = self.conv1(x)\n        x = self.relu(x)\n        x = x.view(x.size(0), -1)\n        x = self.fc(x)\n        return x\n\nmodel = MyModel()  # Or load: model.load_state_dict(torch.load('your_model.pt'))\nmodel.eval()\n\n# Example input (match your model's expected input shape, e.g., batch=1, channels=3, height=224, width=224)\nx = torch.rand(1, 3, 640, 640)\n\n# Trace to TorchScript\ntraced_model = torch.jit.trace(model, x)\ntraced_model.save('model.pt')  # Save traced .pt","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# **2. **Convert to NCNN:****","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Use pnnx to export directly to NCNN\npnnx.export('model.pt',\n            '/kaggle/working/model.ncnn', inputs_shape=[1, 3, 640, 640])  # Adjust input shape","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"This generates model.ncnn.param (model structure) and model.ncnn.bin (weights).\nCommand-line alternative: pnnx model.pt inputshape=[1,3,224,224].\nTips:\nSpecify fp16=1 for half-precision if your model supports it (faster on Pi 5).\nIf errors occur (e.g., unsupported ops), check pnnx docs for workarounds like custom passes.\n\n\n# **3. **Verify Conversion:****\n\nRun a simple forward pass in PyTorch and NCNN to compare outputs (use NCNN's C++ API or Python bindings).","metadata":{}}]}